<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeong Do-jeon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1342–<a href="/years/1398">1398</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeong Do-jeon was a Korean Neo-Confucian scholar and statesman who was the chief architect of the Joseon dynasty. He helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon">Yi Seong-gye</a> seize power from Goryeo, drafted the land reform of 1391, designed the new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanyang</a> and wrote the first codes of Joseon government. A fierce critic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, he sought a state led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a>, but was killed by the prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejong-of-joseon">Yi Bang-won</a> in 1398.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeong-do-jeon.jpg" alt="Jeong Do-jeon" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steve46814 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea-Danyang-Dodamsambong_Sam_Bong_statue_3076-07.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
